WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:14.083
KYB Evidence Desk: explainable EU counterparty checks, anchored on-chain with Chainlink CRE. This video was produced by an AI agent, Grok Bot, operated by Nathaniel. The voice is synthetic; every screen is a real capture.

00:14.533 --> 00:29.128
Before paying a supplier, EU firms must check who they deal with: AML due diligence, VAT registration, verification of payee. But the proof is usually a screenshot. When an auditor asks what was checked, when, and why, there is no evidence.

00:29.578 --> 00:41.606
KYB Evidence Desk turns a VAT number, an IBAN and a claimed name into an evidence report: live checks, sources with hashes, an explainable verdict, the legal basis of each check, and a human review flag.

00:42.056 --> 00:47.001
Live demo: Google Ireland's VAT number, an Irish IBAN, and the claimed name.

00:47.451 --> 00:56.351
Verdict: pass, with stable reason codes. Even a pass recommends a human sign-off, as GDPR article twenty-two requires for decisions with legal effect.

00:56.801 --> 01:03.558
Every check shows its result, source, retrieval time, and the SHA-256 of the raw registry response.

01:04.008 --> 01:12.622
Each check is mapped to its legal basis: AML, VAT and VIES rules, the instant payments regulation, and GDPR, with EUR-Lex links.

01:13.072 --> 01:20.474
A valid Irish company with a German IBAN: a classic invoice redirection signal. Verdict: review, human review required.

01:20.924 --> 01:26.851
A bad IBAN checksum gives fail. The busy French registry is marked unavailable, never a false invalid.

01:27.301 --> 01:31.725
Reports are tamper evident: the server recomputes the canonical hashes, and they match.

01:32.175 --> 01:35.878
Change the decision from pass to fail, and verification returns false.

01:36.328 --> 01:45.181
AI agents use the compact attestation endpoint. It holds only hashes, the decision and the day, so independent nodes compute byte-identical results.

01:45.631 --> 02:00.969
Architecture: a Cloudflare worker runs the checks. A Chainlink CRE workflow has every DON node call the API, requires identical results, signs a report, and writes it to a registry contract on Ethereum Sepolia. Only hashes go on-chain.

02:01.419 --> 02:10.521
The CRE handler, in the TypeScript SDK: HTTP fetch, consensus by fields with identical, a signed report, and an EVM write report.

02:10.971 --> 02:17.775
Tests run the full handler with the SDK capability mocks, and the official CRE command line compiles it to WebAssembly.

02:18.225 --> 02:27.205
In a live dry run, four emulated nodes query the live API, agree byte for byte, and the encoded report goes to the registry. The chain write is mocked here.

02:27.655 --> 02:33.304
When registries disagree, consensus fails and nothing is written. The workflow fails closed.

02:33.754 --> 02:42.717
Sourced, explainable, human-reviewed, provable. Open source, MIT. Built, and this video produced, by an AI agent operated by Nathaniel.
